    "turn_idx": 34,
    "turn_text": "Oh, I'm feeling really good. You know, this started back in February, routine mammogram. I put it off through the pandemic, like so many women have actually believed thousands of women have undetected breast cancer right now. But I finally had it and they found it early enough. And we're able to remove the lump and it was stage one. I had radiation in May. And then have a very good prognosis I think my chances of getting cancer again are about the same as anyone in the general population. So that's good. But what I've learned through this journey was one one out of three Americans have put off just routine exams, not just for cancer. And that's really scary because a lot of these things, if you catch them early, you're much better off. And then secondly, just what a gift every day is, including everything from my husband getting up early for five days and driving me to radiation treatment or the nurses at Mayo Clinic giving me a red, white and blue mask. when it was all done, or just perfect strangers who had no idea what was going on when I slept back and forth between Minnesota and Washington, D.C. And little did they know I wasn't supposed to lift my suitcase up after that surgery, but they did. And they would just go, hey, I'm looking kind of for Lauren. They don't know who I am because you got masks on the airplane. And they'd always be jumping up and getting out my luggage. So, you know, I had many things happen like that, including my colleagues. It was a tough year. My dad died a lot going on.",
