  },
  {
    "episode_id": 96863,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96863.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 64,
    "turn_text": "Okay, as my daughter would say, that question was a lot. So let's start back with what's happened with the court, how we ended up where we are. You know, and I predicted this, that these Trump justices, three of them adding to the two conservatives that are already on the court. And then you have Justice Roberts, who of course is conservative. But in this case, in my mind, did the right thing, even though he may disagree in his own mind of some of the issues, he did the right thing under the law. So you had that five-judgment majority in the middle of the night with, in Justice Kagan's words, less than 72 hours of thought, and they green-lighted in her word this law that clearly is unconstitutional under Roe v. Wade, instead of giving what is called an emergency stay, and They've done that in 2015 and 2019 on abortion cases, one of them involving Texas for egregious laws that were just as bad, but this one to me is even worse and I'll get to that. Yeah, so that's what happened. and the court didn't give this day, and now it's taking effect. You ask, is this just about Texas? It's not just about Texas, because you already have governors and conservative legislators in predictable states already saying, oh, we're going to try this now. We already have Mississippi. The Texas law says, basically, when you can hear a heartbeat, basically in six weeks. And if you're seeking abortion services and you don't get it, by then, then you can't get it or you can be reported and get in trouble. So what does that mean? As you said, well, over 90% of women just trying to exercise their constitutional rights will be denied that in Texas. And if other states, which we expect will happen, do identical laws, it's going to multiply across the country. So why this law is so bad, which I just explained, is they've created bounty hunters in Texas. They basically have said, oh, well, if you find out a woman is seeking an abortion, you can report her and get 10,000 bucks. That means a neighbor that happens to overhear something. That means someone who hears a private conversation at a coffee shop. That could mean someone who drove someone. That could mean someone who's spying on an abortion clinic or any kind of health clinic that provides abortions. It's just simply outrageous. And the fact, and even Justice Roberts in his own opinion, In this case, he said it was highly unusual and unprecedented, and that basically Texas was ceding their power to enforce the laws to the general populace. Those are Justice Roberts words, and he is not exactly a flaming liberal.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_04",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The Texas law prohibits abortion once a fetal heartbeat is detectable, approximately six weeks into pregnancy.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "The Supreme Court issued a decision in the middle of the night with less than 72 hours of deliberation.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Justice Roberts described the Texas law as 'highly unusual and unprecedented' in its delegation of enforcement to private citizens.",
