  {
    "episode_id": 96863,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96863.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 78,
    "turn_text": "Welcome back to just something about her with Senator Amy Klobuchar, who was just making her case as to why she thinks Congress should get rid of the filibuster altogether. So I was surprised to see that for Senator Amy Klobuchar, you represent a relatively conservative state. You have a strong record of having worked bipartisan across the aisle, try to get things done with the other party, go out of your way in your own state to really reach across party lines. And you had a tweet on September 6, this is shortly after the Supreme Court allowed the Texas state to take a fact, Texas state law to take a fact. And you said, it's pretty simple. Unless we get rid of the filibuster, we're going to sit around admiring a lot of problems. I love that. And sending a lot of tweets. Instead, we should get something done. I feel like this is a pretty big statement for you. Right? I mean, what was this journey like for you? Good question. How do you make the case for it?",
