        "text": "Problem-solving is a primary measure of legislative effectiveness.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Public perception of Congress is influenced by visible progress rather than institutional processes.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Restoring faith in Congress requires demonstrable outcomes, not just intentions or debates.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "People value representation and action over ideological purity or procedural correctness.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Bipartisanship is seen as legitimate and valuable in addressing national issues.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 96863,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96863.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 96,
    "turn_text": "We demonstrated as I noted a few times the last year when we had to respond and we did in major ways to keep the economy going and to get the vaccines distributed and we even did that last year. Sometimes without any leadership from the White House, we kept going and now we have. Joe Biden in and he is leading the administration in a way to me that is much more competent and we are moving ahead. So I just refuse to give up on America here. I think that despite the polarization that there are ways to find common ground, but I refuse to. allow ancient procedural rules that were done to not get certain things done as in civil rights legislation that were put in place to define our future.",
