        "text": "Bipartisanship was once achievable in the past.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The current political environment lacks incentives for cooperation between parties.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "Recreating historical conditions requires understanding their underlying mechanisms.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker has insight into how political systems function at a structural level.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "The listener possesses relevant personal experience with past bipartisan periods.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Clarification Request (Specific)"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 96863,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96863.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 116,
    "turn_text": "Democrats are still kind of traditional, like, to get things done, and Republicans only want to appeal to their base, and those things are odds. But what I've heard her say, and even some Republicans I've talked to about it, is there's enough Republicans in Congress that do want to show they can get something done. I'm an optimistic person by nature, so take what I say with a grain of salt. the fact that you have Amy Klobuchar, you know, she's like very pragmatic, that she both wants to work in a bipartisan way and things we need to either get rid of or reform the filibuster. That suggests to me that both of those things may actually happen.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_06",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "Amy Klobuchar is pragmatic and seeks bipartisan cooperation.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "Democrats are somewhat traditional in their approach to getting things done.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "Republicans primarily aim to appeal to their base rather than achieve broad consensus.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The Democratic and Republican approaches are fundamentally at odds.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "Some Republicans in Congress want to demonstrate they can get things done.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Both bipartisanship and filibuster reform are potentially achievable.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      },
      {
        "text": "Reform or elimination of the filibuster is necessary.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Pragmatism in politics involves working across party lines.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "A willingness to compromise indicates genuine desire to accomplish legislative goals.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The existence of moderate Republicans signals potential for meaningful political change.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "Bipartisan success depends on having influential figures who prioritize action over ideology.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker’s optimism does not undermine the credibility of their assessment.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      },
      {
        "text": "Amy Klobuchar represents a credible model of effective bipartisan leadership.",
        "confidence": 0.86
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 96863,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/6/96863.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 117,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, and she debunked the idea that getting rid of the filibuster would mean that there's no bipartisanship. She said there's still going to be people working together to chip away at different parts of each of the bills. So that was interesting to hear as well.",
